window.CourseData=window.CourseData||{};window.CourseData["arc-flash-nfpa70e"]={"slug":"arc-flash-nfpa70e","title":"Arc Flash Safety and NFPA 70E (2024 Edition)","short":"Arc Flash & NFPA 70E","hours":4,"edition":0,"scope":"multi","audience":"Journeyman & Master Electrician","articles":"NFPA 70E, OSHA Subpart S","free":false,"labs":[],"slideMin":150,"modules":[{"title":"Electrical Safety Fundamentals and NFPA 70E","minutes":60,"overview":["How OSHA, NFPA 70E and the NEC fit together","What current does to the body and what an arc flash is","Hierarchy of risk controls and the electrical safety program","Who is a qualified person, training and audits","Edition note: 70E 2024 and what is changing in 2027"],"slides":[{"title":"Three documents, three jobs","body":"OSHA, NFPA 70E and the NEC are often mixed up. They do different jobs, and a safe electrician knows which one answers which question.","keypoint":"NFPA 70E is not a federal law. OSHA can still cite an employer for electrical hazards, and an employer who ignores 70E has a hard time showing it protected workers by some other recognized method.","def_grid":[{"term":"OSHA (29 CFR)","body":"The law. It sets what employers must do. Subpart S (1910.301-.399) covers electrical work in general industry; Subpart K (1926.400-.449) covers construction."},{"term":"NFPA 70E","body":"A consensus standard on electrical safety in the workplace: how to work safely around electrical hazards. OSHA does not adopt it by reference, but it is widely used to show how to meet OSHA's general safety duties."},{"term":"NEC (NFPA 70)","body":"The installation code: how electrical systems must be designed and installed. It says what to build; 70E says how to work on it safely."}],"refs":["OSHA 29 CFR 1910 Subpart S","OSHA 29 CFR 1926 Subpart K","NFPA 70E Art. 90"],"checks":[{"q":"Which document is the federal regulation that employers must follow for electrical work in general industry?","opts":["OSHA 29 CFR 1910 Subpart S","NFPA 70E","NFPA 70 (the NEC)","The manufacturer installation instructions"],"correct":0,"explain":"OSHA rules are law. 70E is a consensus standard, and the NEC is an installation code.","ref":"1910 Subpart S"}]},{"title":"What current does to the body","body":"Electric shock injury depends on how much current flows through the body, by what path, and for how long. Voltage matters because it drives the current through the body's resistance.","info":"These are commonly cited approximate ranges. Individual response varies. Treat any shock as serious, because the heart can be affected hours later.","bullets":["Dry skin may have thousands of ohms of resistance; wet or broken skin can be far lower, so the same voltage can drive far more current.","Current across the chest (hand to hand, hand to foot) is the most dangerous path.","Even 120 V can kill. Many fatal shocks involve ordinary voltages."],"table":{"h":["Approximate current (60 Hz, hand to foot)","Typical effect"],"r":[["About 1 mA","Barely perceptible tingle"],["About 5 to 10 mA","Painful; muscles may contract. Many people cannot let go above roughly 10 mA"],["About 30 mA and up","Breathing can stop if current continues"],["About 50 to 100 mA and up","Ventricular fibrillation is possible. The heart stops pumping blood"],["Several hundred mA and up","Severe burns and internal injury"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E informative annexes on electrical hazards","OSHA Electrical Hazards eTool"],"checks":[{"q":"About how much current across the chest can start ventricular fibrillation?","opts":["50 to 100 mA","1 mA","About 0.1 mA","Only currents above 10 A"],"correct":0,"explain":"Tens of milliamps across the chest can disrupt the heart rhythm. 1 mA is only perceptible.","ref":""}]},{"title":"What an arc flash is","body":"An arc flash is a release of energy caused by an electric arc: current jumping through the air between conductors, or from a conductor to ground. It can start from a dropped tool, a loose connection, contamination, a failed component, or a racking mishap.","keypoint":"Burns, not shock, cause most arc flash injuries. The arc can be a bigger risk to a worker than touching a live part.","bullets":["Arc temperatures can reach tens of thousands of degrees, hotter than the surface of the sun.","The arc vaporizes metal. Copper expands enormously as it turns to vapor, which drives a pressure wave called arc blast.","Hazards include burns, flying shrapnel and molten metal, pressure injury, intense light, and very loud noise.","Injury from an arc can happen several feet away. Distance helps, but it does not remove the hazard."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 100 (Arc Flash Hazard)","NFPA 70E Informative Annex D"],"checks":[{"q":"Arc blast is mainly caused by","opts":["Rapid expansion of vaporized metal and heated air","Static electricity","Voltage drop across the arc","Reflected light"],"correct":0,"explain":"Metal turns to vapor and air heats suddenly, creating a pressure wave.","ref":""}]},{"title":"What changes the severity of an arc flash","body":"Two arc flashes in the same room can be wildly different. The incident energy a worker receives depends on a handful of factors you can often influence.","keypoint":"Time is the factor engineers can change most easily. Fast protection means lower incident energy.","table":{"h":["Factor","Effect on incident energy"],"r":[["Clearing time of the upstream protective device","Directly proportional. Half the time is roughly half the energy at the same current."],["Arcing current (related to available fault current)","Higher arcing current usually means more energy, but a very high current can trip a current-limiting device faster."],["Working distance","Energy falls quickly as you move away from the arc."],["Equipment type and enclosure size","Box size and electrode arrangement change how energy is directed."],["Voltage and gap between conductors","Higher voltage and larger gaps change arc behavior."]]},"refs":["IEEE 1584-2018","NFPA 70E Annex D"],"checks":[{"q":"A protective device clears a fault in 0.5 s. Its settings are changed so it clears the same fault in 0.1 s. The incident energy is about","opts":["One fifth as much","Five times as much","The same","Double"],"correct":0,"explain":"At the same arcing current, energy is roughly proportional to arcing time, so 0.1 s is one fifth of 0.5 s.","ref":""}]},{"title":"The hierarchy of risk controls","body":"Electrical safety decisions should start with the strongest control and work down only when a stronger control is not possible. PPE is last, because it protects after the hazard is already present.","warning":"Starting with \"what PPE do I need?\" skips the best controls. Ask first whether the equipment can be de-energized.","hierarchy":[{"n":1,"title":"Elimination","sub":"Remove the hazard. Establish an electrically safe work condition by de-energizing.","bg":"#F0FDF4","bc":"#16A34A","tc":"#14532D"},{"n":2,"title":"Substitution","sub":"Replace with a less hazardous option, for example a lower-energy device or a remote operating method.","bg":"#EFF6FF","bc":"#2563EB","tc":"#1E40AF"},{"n":3,"title":"Engineering controls","sub":"Arc-resistant equipment, remote racking, current-limiting fuses, arc energy reduction maintenance modes, barriers.","bg":"#F0F9FF","bc":"#0369A1","tc":"#0C4A6E"},{"n":4,"title":"Awareness","sub":"Labels, signs, barricades and warnings that make the hazard visible.","bg":"#FEFCE8","bc":"#CA8A04","tc":"#713F12"},{"n":5,"title":"Administrative controls","sub":"Procedures, work permits, job briefings, training, limiting who may enter an area.","bg":"#FFF7ED","bc":"#C2410C","tc":"#431407"},{"n":6,"title":"PPE","sub":"Arc-rated clothing, face and head protection, insulating gloves. The last line of defense.","bg":"#FEF2F2","bc":"#DC2626","tc":"#7F1D1D"}],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 110","NFPA 70E Informative Annex F"],"checks":[{"q":"In the hierarchy of risk controls, which comes first?","opts":["Elimination of the hazard","Arc-rated PPE","A warning label","Training"],"correct":0,"explain":"Eliminating the hazard, usually by de-energizing, is the strongest control. PPE is last.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Employer and employee responsibilities","body":"The employer sets up and runs the electrical safety program and provides what workers need. Workers follow the program and speak up when it is not working.","bullets":["Employer: establish, document and implement an electrical safety program, including policies, procedures and a hazard review.","Employer: provide training, appropriate tools and PPE, and make sure workers are qualified for the tasks they do.","Employer: tell contractors about known hazards, and get hazard information from contractors; host and contract employers coordinate.","Employee: follow the procedures and training; use the PPE and tools provided; report hazards and damaged equipment.","OSHA requires employers to provide most required PPE at no cost to employees, with limited exceptions such as ordinary non-specialty items."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 105","NFPA 70E Art. 110","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.132"],"checks":[{"q":"Who is responsible for setting up and documenting the electrical safety program?","opts":["The employer","The electrical inspector","The equipment manufacturer","Each employee individually"],"correct":0,"explain":"The employer is responsible; employees follow and support it.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Who is a qualified person","body":"A qualified person has skills and knowledge related to the construction and operation of the specific equipment and has been trained to identify and avoid the hazards involved. Qualification is not a blanket status.","keypoint":"A license alone does not make someone qualified under 70E. The employer must train the person on the specific hazards and tasks.","def_grid":[{"term":"Qualified person","body":"Trained and able to do the work and to recognize and avoid the electrical hazards on the specific equipment and task."},{"term":"Unqualified person","body":"Not trained for the specific hazards. May not cross the limited approach boundary unless advised and escorted by a qualified person."},{"term":"Task and equipment specific","body":"A person may be qualified for some equipment and tasks, and not for others. Experience on a 208 V panel does not qualify someone for medium-voltage switchgear."}],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 100 (Qualified Person)","NFPA 70E Art. 110"],"checks":[{"q":"An electrician is fully qualified on 480 V panelboards. Is that person automatically qualified on medium-voltage switchgear?","opts":["No, qualification depends on the specific equipment and tasks","Yes, voltage does not matter","Yes, if licensed","Yes, after one hour of review"],"correct":0,"explain":"Qualification is specific to the equipment, hazards and tasks.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Training, retraining and audits","body":"The employer must train workers and keep the program current. The standard sets intervals for retraining and audits.","info":"Audit intervals vary between editions and between the standard and OSHA lockout rules. Use the edition your employer has adopted.","table":{"h":["Item","Requirement (verify against your edition)"],"r":[["Initial training","Before a person works where electrical hazards exist at the level of exposure"],["Retraining","At intervals not exceeding three years, and sooner if practices are deficient, procedures change, or new equipment or tasks arise"],["Training documentation","Records should show content, dates and who was trained"],["Electrical safety program audit","Intervals not exceeding three years"],["Field work audit","Annual audit of work practices in the field"],["Lockout/tagout procedure and program audits","Procedures audited at least annually; see your edition for the program audit interval"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 110","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147(c)(6)"]},{"title":"Emergency response and job safety planning","body":"Before electrical work starts, workers need to know how to get help. The standard expects an emergency response plan, including how to release a person from contact, who can give first aid, and how to reach responders.","warning":"Do not touch a victim who may still be in contact with an energized source. De-energize or use proper insulating equipment to separate them first.","bullets":["A person who works within the limited approach boundary should be trained in first aid and CPR/AED as the standard requires.","Know the way to release a victim from contact: de-energize first when possible; do not touch a person who is still in contact.","Know the location of the nearest AED and emergency shutoff.","Hold a job briefing before the work, covering hazards, procedures, energy source controls, PPE and emergency plan.","Brief again when the job changes."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 110","NFPA 70E Art. 130"],"checks":[{"q":"A coworker is frozen to an energized conductor. What is the first priority?","opts":["Stop the current flow (de-energize) and call for help, without touching the person","Pull the person off by hand immediately","Throw water on the person","Wait to see if they let go"],"correct":0,"explain":"Touching the victim can make you part of the circuit.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Edition note: 70E 2024 and 2027","body":"NFPA 70E is revised every three years. This course follows the 2024 edition as the base. A 2027 edition has been prepared, and this slide summarizes reported changes from secondary sources, so confirm against the published text.","warning":"Section numbers move between editions. Cite the edition your employer has adopted, and check the actual text before relying on a number from any summary, including this one.","bullets":["2024 reported changes: definitions consolidated in Article 100; Article 110 policy and emergency response language; Article 120 testing requirements clarified; restricted approach boundaries in the table aligned with OSHA 1910.269 values; labels must be durable; new Informative Annex S on maintenance condition.","2027 reported changes (secondary sources): extra person for certain justified energized work, additional testing when voltage testing alone is not enough (for example current transformer secondaries), expanded battery rules, and new Chapter 3 articles including photovoltaic systems.","Reported change to lockout/tagout program audit intervals in 2027 (from one year to three) should be confirmed in the published text."],"refs":["NFPA 70E (2024)","NFPA 70E (2027)"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"NFPA 70E is best described as","opts":["A consensus standard on electrical safety in the workplace","A federal law","An installation code for wiring methods","A UL product standard"],"correct":0,"explain":"70E is a consensus standard. OSHA is the regulation; the NEC is the installation code.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 90"},{"q":"The NEC mostly addresses","opts":["How electrical installations are designed and installed","How to perform work safely on energized equipment","Employer lockout procedures","PPE care"],"correct":0,"explain":"The NEC is an installation code; 70E covers work practices.","ref":"NFPA 70"},{"q":"OSHA electrical rules for general industry are found in","opts":["29 CFR 1910 Subpart S","29 CFR 1926 Subpart A","NFPA 70E Chapter 1 only","The NEC Article 90"],"correct":0,"explain":"Subpart S is the general industry electrical standard; 1926 Subpart K covers construction.","ref":"1910 Subpart S"},{"q":"Which of the following is a commonly cited threshold at which many people cannot let go of a conductor?","opts":["About 10 mA","About 0.01 mA","About 1 A","About 100 A"],"correct":0,"explain":"Let-go thresholds are roughly in the 10 mA range, with wide variation.","ref":""},{"q":"Which injury type most often results from arc flash exposure?","opts":["Burns","Frostbite","Hypothermia","Muscle strain only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Thermal burns dominate arc flash injuries, along with pressure and shrapnel injury.","ref":""},{"q":"Arc blast is","opts":["A pressure wave from rapidly expanding vaporized metal and heated air","A fuse operating normally","A voltage dip","A grounded conductor"],"correct":0,"explain":"The pressure wave can throw workers and equipment.","ref":""},{"q":"Incident energy at a given arcing current is most directly proportional to","opts":["Arcing time","Conductor color","Phase sequence","Nameplate manufacturer"],"correct":0,"explain":"Longer clearing time means more energy delivered.","ref":"IEEE 1584"},{"q":"Which is an engineering control?","opts":["A remote racking device for a circuit breaker","A warning sign","A written procedure","Arc-rated clothing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Remote racking increases distance and reduces exposure. Signs are awareness; procedures are administrative; clothing is PPE.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex F"},{"q":"Which is an administrative control?","opts":["An energized electrical work permit","A current-limiting fuse","Arc-resistant switchgear","A barrier"],"correct":0,"explain":"Permits and procedures are administrative controls.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex F"},{"q":"Where does PPE fall in the hierarchy of risk controls?","opts":["Last","First","Second","Not part of it"],"correct":0,"explain":"PPE is the least effective control because it does not remove the hazard.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex F"},{"q":"What is the first control to consider for a piece of equipment that can be de-energized?","opts":["De-energize it and establish an electrically safe work condition","Put on category 4 PPE","Post a sign","Work faster"],"correct":0,"explain":"Elimination first.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"A qualified person is one who","opts":["Has the skills and training for the specific equipment, task and hazards","Holds any electrical license","Has worked for the employer for a year","Is a supervisor"],"correct":0,"explain":"Qualification is specific; a license alone is not enough.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 100"},{"q":"An unqualified person may cross the limited approach boundary only if","opts":["Advised and escorted by a qualified person","They wear a hard hat","They are in a hurry","The equipment is labeled"],"correct":0,"explain":"Unqualified persons need an escort and the right instructions.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"Retraining under 70E is expected at intervals not exceeding","opts":["Three years","Ten years","Six months","Never, once qualified"],"correct":0,"explain":"Retraining is required at least every three years and when conditions change.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"The electrical safety program audit interval in 70E is","opts":["Not to exceed three years","Every ten years","Only after an injury","Monthly"],"correct":0,"explain":"The program audit is on a three-year cycle, with annual field work audits.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"Who must tell a contractor about known electrical hazards at a host site?","opts":["The host employer","The inspector","The utility only","No one"],"correct":0,"explain":"Host and contract employers must exchange hazard information.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"A job briefing should be held","opts":["Before work starts, and again when the job changes","Only after the job","Only for new employees","Only for energized work over 600 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"Briefings cover hazards, procedures, controls, PPE and emergency plans.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"A fellow worker is stuck to an energized conductor. You should","opts":["De-energize the source and call for help rather than touch the person","Pull them away with bare hands","Pour water on them","Ignore until they let go"],"correct":0,"explain":"Touching the victim can make you part of the circuit.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Shock Hazards, Approach Boundaries and Arc Flash Risk Assessment","minutes":60,"overview":["Limited, restricted and arc flash boundaries","The shock risk assessment","The arc flash risk assessment: incident energy and PPE category methods","Labels and reviewing the analysis","Engineering controls that lower the hazard"],"slides":[{"title":"Three boundaries to know","body":"Before work begins you need to know where the shock and arc flash boundaries are. They are distances from exposed energized parts that tell you who may enter and what protection is needed.","info":"The prohibited approach boundary was removed from the standard in the 2015 edition. Do not teach it as current.","def_grid":[{"term":"Limited approach boundary","body":"A shock boundary. Unqualified persons must not cross it unless advised and escorted by a qualified person."},{"term":"Restricted approach boundary","body":"A closer shock boundary where the risk of shock from movement is much higher. Only qualified persons, with a plan and appropriate protection, may cross it."},{"term":"Arc flash boundary","body":"The distance at which a person without PPE would receive a second-degree burn, at an incident energy of 1.2 cal/cm2 (5 J/cm2)."}],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130","NFPA 70E Art. 100"],"checks":[{"q":"The arc flash boundary is the distance where unprotected skin could receive incident energy of about","opts":["1.2 cal/cm2","12 cal/cm2","0.1 cal/cm2","40 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"1.2 cal/cm2 is the onset of a second-degree burn.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Shock approach boundary values","body":"Shock boundaries depend on system voltage and whether the conductor is movable or fixed. Table 130.4(E)(a) lists the values for AC systems.","info":"These are the commonly published values for these two ranges. Higher voltage ranges have their own values; use the current table in your edition for any real work. Editions adjust some rounding.","bullets":["The limited approach boundary for movable conductors is larger because the conductor can move toward you.","Elevation can change boundary distances in some voltage ranges, so check the table notes."],"table":{"h":["Nominal system voltage (phase to phase)","Limited approach, exposed movable conductor","Limited approach, exposed fixed part","Restricted approach"],"r":[["50 V to 150 V","10 ft 0 in (3.0 m)","3 ft 6 in (1.0 m)","Avoid contact"],["151 V to 750 V","10 ft 0 in (3.0 m)","3 ft 6 in (1.0 m)","1 ft 0 in (0.3 m)"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Table 130.4(E)(a)"],"checks":[{"q":"For a 480 V fixed energized part, the commonly published limited approach boundary is","opts":["3 ft 6 in","10 ft 0 in","1 ft 0 in","25 ft"],"correct":0,"explain":"For 151 to 750 V, the limited approach boundary for a fixed part is 3 ft 6 in. The 10 ft value applies to exposed movable conductors.","ref":"Table 130.4(E)(a)"}]},{"title":"Crossing the restricted approach boundary","body":"Entering the restricted approach boundary is an elevated-risk act. Only a qualified person may do it, and the standard attaches conditions.","warning":"Unqualified persons cannot enter the restricted approach boundary under any circumstance.","info":"The conditions are summarized in plain language. Verify the exact wording in Article 130 of the edition your employer has adopted.","step_list":["A documented plan is in place and approved by the authorized person.","The qualified person uses PPE appropriate for the task, including shock protection.","The worker is positioned and protected so inadvertent movement cannot cause contact with energized parts.","An energized electrical work permit is completed when the task requires one."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130"],"checks":[{"q":"Who may cross the restricted approach boundary?","opts":["Only a qualified person with the proper plan and protection","Anyone with a hard hat","An unqualified person with an escort","Any supervisor"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only qualified persons, with controls, may cross.","ref":""}]},{"title":"The shock risk assessment","body":"A shock risk assessment looks at the system voltage, the condition of the equipment, the task, and whether the worker may be exposed to energized parts. It tells you what shock PPE and boundaries apply.","bullets":["Identify the nominal voltage the worker could be exposed to.","Determine the boundaries from the table.","Decide whether the worker will be exposed to energized parts, which means shock PPE such as rubber insulating gloves with leather protectors.","Identify any added tasks that increase exposure, for example opening a cover or reaching past an unguarded part.","Document the assessment where required."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130","NFPA 70E Annex F"],"checks":[{"q":"A shock risk assessment is used to determine","opts":["What shock protection and boundaries are needed for the task","The installation date","The color of the gloves","The brand of meter"],"correct":0,"explain":"It sets boundaries and PPE for the shock hazard.","ref":""}]},{"title":"The arc flash risk assessment","body":"The arc flash risk assessment decides whether an arc flash hazard exists and what protection the worker needs. The standard gives two methods for selecting arc flash PPE and an arc flash boundary.","warning":"Do not mix methods on the same piece of equipment. Choose either the incident energy value or the PPE category, not both.","table":{"h":["Method","How it works","Use it when"],"r":[["Incident energy analysis method","An engineering study (often IEEE 1584-2018 based) calculates incident energy at the working distance for each piece of equipment.","Equipment is outside the limits of the category tables, or the study already exists"],["Arc flash PPE categories method","Uses tables of tasks and equipment to assign category 1 to 4 PPE, with the arc flash boundary from tables, within stated limits for available fault current and clearing time.","Equipment falls within the table parameters"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.5","NFPA 70E Annex D","IEEE 1584-2018"],"checks":[{"q":"Which method uses an engineering study to calculate incident energy at the working distance?","opts":["Incident energy analysis method","PPE categories method","Shock risk assessment","Hierarchy of controls"],"correct":0,"explain":"The incident energy analysis method calculates values for each piece of equipment.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Incident energy analysis in practice","body":"A study uses system data: available fault current, protective device settings and clearing times, equipment type, enclosure size, gap, voltage and working distance. The result is incident energy and an arc flash boundary per piece of equipment.","info":"The incident energy and arc flash PPE category methods are alternatives. A label will show one or the other, not both.","bullets":["Inputs must reflect the system as built. Old or wrong one-line data produces a wrong study.","The result is only as good as the clearing time assumed. A device that fails to trip or is set wrong changes the answer.","The incident energy tells you the minimum arc rating of clothing needed at the working distance, in cal/cm2.","A study should be reviewed when equipment or settings change, and at intervals not exceeding five years."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.5","IEEE 1584-2018"],"checks":[{"q":"How often must the arc flash risk assessment be reviewed for accuracy, at the longest?","opts":["At intervals not exceeding five years","Every ten years","Only after an arc flash","Annually, no exceptions"],"correct":0,"explain":"The standard requires review at least every five years and when major modifications occur.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.5"}]},{"title":"Arc flash PPE categories","body":"The PPE categories method assigns arc flash PPE categories 1 through 4 based on the equipment and task, using minimum arc ratings.","keypoint":"There is no category 0. It was removed from the standard in the 2015 edition. When the hazard is low enough that no arc rating is needed, the clothing should still be non-melting.","table":{"h":["Arc flash PPE category","Minimum arc rating of clothing"],"r":[["1","4 cal/cm2"],["2","8 cal/cm2"],["3","25 cal/cm2"],["4","40 cal/cm2"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130 (arc flash PPE categories table)"],"checks":[{"q":"What is the minimum arc rating for arc flash PPE category 2?","opts":["8 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2","25 cal/cm2","40 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Categories 1 through 4 are 4, 8, 25 and 40 cal/cm2.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Likelihood and severity","body":"The standard uses tasks and equipment conditions to decide whether an arc flash is likely when a task is performed. Operating condition matters: equipment that is normal, properly installed and maintained, and with covers in place has a lower likelihood than equipment with abnormal conditions.","bullets":["Tasks such as racking a breaker, opening a cover, or working inside a panel with the door open may carry higher likelihood.","Equipment that is not properly maintained, is damaged, or has loose parts has higher likelihood.","The risk assessment should consider both the likelihood of an incident and the severity of injury if it happens.","Wearing PPE reduces injury severity; it does not reduce likelihood."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Table 130.5(C)"],"checks":[{"q":"A closed, properly maintained panel versus a worn, open panel: which has the higher likelihood of an arc flash during a task?","opts":["The worn, open panel","The closed panel","They are always equal","Neither, arc flash is random"],"correct":0,"explain":"Condition and task both change likelihood.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Equipment labeling","body":"Equipment likely to be examined, adjusted, serviced or maintained while energized must be field-marked with an arc flash label. The label must be durable for its environment.","info":"The NEC separately requires arc-flash warning labels on certain equipment under NEC 110.16, and marking of available fault current at service equipment under NEC 110.24.","table":{"h":["Label content (70E 130.5(H))","Notes"],"r":[["Nominal system voltage","Always required"],["Arc flash boundary","Always required"],["At least one of: available incident energy with working distance, or arc flash PPE category with minimum arc rating, or site-specific PPE level","Do not mix incident energy and PPE category on one label"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E 130.5(H)","NEC 110.16","NEC 110.24"],"checks":[{"q":"Which two items must always appear on an arc flash label under 70E?","opts":["Nominal system voltage and arc flash boundary","Manufacturer and date","Serial number and panel color","Cost and installer"],"correct":0,"explain":"The label must show voltage and boundary, plus one of the three PPE or energy options.","ref":"130.5(H)"}]},{"title":"Reducing the hazard with engineering controls","body":"Engineering controls lower the energy an arc can release and are higher in the hierarchy than PPE.","bullets":["Faster clearing: relays and breakers set to trip faster, zone-selective interlocking, maintenance mode switches that temporarily lower settings (arc energy reduction).","Current-limiting fuses and current-limiting breakers limit let-through energy.","Remote operation and remote racking increase the distance between worker and equipment.","Arc-resistant switchgear directs blast energy away from the operator.","The NEC requires arc energy reduction means for circuit breakers rated 1200 A or higher (NEC 240.87).","Infrared windows and online monitoring reduce the need to open energized equipment."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Annex F","NEC 240.87"],"checks":[{"q":"A maintenance mode switch that lowers protective device trip settings is an example of","opts":["An engineering control that reduces arcing time","Administrative control","PPE","A label"],"correct":0,"explain":"It reduces clearing time and so incident energy.","ref":"NEC 240.87"}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"The limited approach boundary is mainly a","opts":["Shock boundary","Arc flash boundary","Fire boundary","PPE boundary"],"correct":0,"explain":"Limited and restricted are shock boundaries; the arc flash boundary is separate.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"Who may cross the limited approach boundary without an escort?","opts":["A qualified person","Any visitor","Any apprentice","Anyone with a hard hat"],"correct":0,"explain":"Unqualified persons need an escort.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"The prohibited approach boundary","opts":["Was removed in the 2015 edition","Is in every edition","Is in the 2024 edition only","Is the same as the arc flash boundary"],"correct":0,"explain":"It was dropped; the standard now uses limited, restricted and arc flash boundaries.","ref":"NFPA 70E"},{"q":"The arc flash boundary is the distance at which a person would receive","opts":["1.2 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2","8 cal/cm2","40 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"1.2 cal/cm2 is the onset of a second-degree burn.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 100"},{"q":"A 480 V system: the restricted approach boundary in the table is","opts":["1 ft 0 in","3 ft 6 in","10 ft","25 ft"],"correct":0,"explain":"For 151 to 750 V it is 1 ft 0 in.","ref":"Table 130.4(E)(a)"},{"q":"A shock risk assessment determines","opts":["Voltage, boundaries and shock PPE needed for the task","The cost of the work","Arc flash incident energy only","The installer name"],"correct":0,"explain":"It addresses the shock hazard, including PPE and boundaries.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"Which method calculates incident energy for the equipment?","opts":["The incident energy analysis method","The PPE categories method","The hierarchy of controls","A shock assessment"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is an engineering calculation.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex D"},{"q":"Which method uses tables to assign category 1 to 4?","opts":["The arc flash PPE categories method","The incident energy method","The energized work permit","The audit"],"correct":0,"explain":"It uses tables with limits on fault current and clearing time.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"Arc flash PPE category 4 has a minimum arc rating of","opts":["40 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2","8 cal/cm2","25 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Category 4 is 40 cal/cm2.","ref":"NFPA 70E"},{"q":"Arc flash PPE category 3 has a minimum arc rating of","opts":["25 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2","8 cal/cm2","40 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Category 3 is 25 cal/cm2.","ref":"NFPA 70E"},{"q":"Arc flash PPE category 0","opts":["Does not exist in current editions","Is 1 cal/cm2","Is for 480 V only","Is the label color"],"correct":0,"explain":"It was removed in 2015.","ref":"NFPA 70E"},{"q":"The arc flash risk assessment must be reviewed at intervals not exceeding","opts":["Five years","One year","Ten years","Twenty years"],"correct":0,"explain":"Five-year review, plus when major modifications are made.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.5"},{"q":"A label shows available incident energy and working distance. Which item should it NOT also show?","opts":["An arc flash PPE category","Nominal system voltage","Arc flash boundary","The working distance"],"correct":0,"explain":"A label uses either incident energy or the PPE category, not both.","ref":"130.5(H)"},{"q":"Which is an engineering control that reduces arc energy?","opts":["A maintenance mode switch lowering trip time","A sign","A procedure","A hard hat"],"correct":0,"explain":"Faster clearing lowers energy.","ref":"NEC 240.87"},{"q":"Arc-resistant switchgear works by","opts":["Directing blast energy away from the operator","Eliminating all voltage","Preventing shock","Replacing PPE"],"correct":0,"explain":"It channels pressure and heat away from the worker.","ref":""},{"q":"Which would most reduce the incident energy of a fault if the arcing current stays the same?","opts":["Clearing the fault in less time","A longer fault duration","A warmer room","A different label color"],"correct":0,"explain":"Energy is proportional to time at a given current.","ref":"IEEE 1584"},{"q":"Wearing PPE reduces","opts":["The severity of an injury, not the likelihood of an incident","The likelihood of an arc","The voltage","The available fault current"],"correct":0,"explain":"PPE only reduces injury severity.","ref":""},{"q":"The NEC requires arc energy reduction for circuit breakers rated at","opts":["1200 A or higher","Any size","100 A","Only 15 A"],"correct":0,"explain":"NEC 240.87 applies at 1200 A or higher.","ref":"NEC 240.87"}]},{"title":"Personal Protective Equipment for Shock and Arc Flash","minutes":60,"overview":["Arc-rated clothing and layering","Head, face, eye, hearing and foot protection","Rubber insulating gloves, sleeves and leather protectors","Insulated tools and test instruments","Inspection, care, testing intervals and PPE selection"],"slides":[{"title":"PPE is the last line of defense","body":"PPE does not prevent an incident. It reduces injury when the hazard is still present. Choose it after a risk assessment, and use it with the other controls.","bullets":["PPE must be rated for the hazard: shock, arc flash, or both.","PPE must fit, be in good condition, and be worn as a system. Gaps defeat the protection.","PPE selection follows the risk assessment: the PPE category or the incident energy value at the working distance.","Wear the PPE for the highest hazard present, not an average."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.132"],"checks":[{"q":"PPE selection for arc flash should be based on","opts":["The arc flash risk assessment results","What the crew had last time","The employee preference","The weight of the equipment"],"correct":0,"explain":"The assessment sets category or incident energy.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Arc-rated clothing","body":"Arc-rated clothing is tested to measure its resistance to an arc exposure. The rating is in cal/cm2 and tells you the exposure at which there is a 50% probability of a second-degree burn through the fabric.","keypoint":"Choose clothing whose arc rating is at least the incident energy at the working distance, or at least the PPE category minimum.","table":{"h":["Term","Meaning"],"r":[["ATPV","Arc Thermal Performance Value: the incident energy that gives a 50% probability of a second-degree burn through fabric that does not break open."],["EBT","Energy of Breakopen Threshold: the incident energy at which the fabric breaks open, for fabrics that break before ATPV is reached."],["Arc rating","The ATPV or EBT in cal/cm2, whichever is lower, shown on the label."]]},"refs":["ASTM F1959","NFPA 70E Art. 130.7"],"checks":[{"q":"Arc rating is shown in","opts":["cal/cm2","Volts","Amps","Ohms"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arc ratings are in calories per square centimeter.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Layering and what not to wear","body":"Layers of arc-rated clothing add protection, and air between layers helps. Clothing under arc-rated garments matters too.","warning":"Synthetic clothing that melts onto skin can make burns far worse. Check the labels.","bullets":["Do not wear clothing made of meltable fibers such as acetate, nylon, polyester, polypropylene or spandex alone or in blends next to the skin where arc-rated protection is needed. Melting fabric can cause deep burns.","Natural fibers such as untreated cotton can burn but do not melt. Arc-rated underlayers are preferred.","Wear arc-rated garments over non-melting underlayers, and button or fasten them completely.","Arc-rated garment ratings are not simply added up when layered; use the system rating from the manufacturer or test data.","Remove jewelry and metal items that can conduct, melt or become shrapnel."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.7(C)(11)"],"checks":[{"q":"Which clothing is most dangerous to wear under arc-rated gear?","opts":["Polyester that melts","Arc-rated cotton","Untreated cotton","Wool that is flame-resistant"],"correct":0,"explain":"Meltable synthetics can adhere to skin.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Head, face and eye protection","body":"Head and face protection must match the exposure. A face shield alone is not enough at higher energies.","bullets":["Use a hard hat rated for the electrical hazard.","Face shields must be arc-rated; a plain polycarbonate shield may melt or crack.","Eye protection remains required under a face shield."],"table":{"h":["Exposure","Typical protection"],"r":[["Lower incident energy","Arc-rated face shield with a wrap-around guard and balaclava or hood as needed, plus safety glasses"],["Higher incident energy (category 3 and 4 range)","Arc-rated flash suit hood, with a window rated for the exposure, hard hat and safety glasses under"],["Shock only","Safety glasses or goggles, and a hard hat rated for electrical work (Class E or G type as appropriate)"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.7","ANSI/ISEA Z89.1","ANSI/ISEA Z87.1"],"checks":[{"q":"A plain clear polycarbonate face shield is acceptable arc flash face protection","opts":["False. It must be arc-rated for the exposure","True always","True above 600 V","True with a mask"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only arc-rated face protection is accepted.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Hearing and foot protection","body":"The pressure wave and noise of an arc can damage hearing. Protective footwear should keep out shock paths and resist the hazards of the job.","bullets":["Use hearing protection, such as ear canal inserts, where arc blast is possible. Earmuffs alone may not fit under a hood.","Leather work boots are generally acceptable. Rubber-soled boots and electrical hazard rated footwear (EH) provide secondary shock protection only.","Do not rely on boots as a primary shock protection; they are not substitutes for gloves."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.7"],"checks":[{"q":"Electrical hazard (EH) rated footwear is","opts":["Secondary shock protection only","The primary shock protection","A substitute for rubber gloves","Not useful at all"],"correct":0,"explain":"EH footwear helps but never replaces rubber gloves.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Rubber insulating gloves","body":"Rubber insulating gloves protect against shock when worn with leather protectors. The glove class is chosen by the voltage of the work.","keypoint":"Leather protectors go over the rubber glove to protect it from cuts, and to give arc flash protection at lower incident energy. Do not wear the rubber gloves without protectors unless the work and rules allow it.","table":{"h":["Class","Maximum use voltage (AC rms)"],"r":[["00","500 V"],["0","1,000 V"],["1","7,500 V"],["2","17,000 V"],["3","26,500 V"],["4","36,000 V"]]},"refs":["ASTM D120","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.137","NFPA 70E Art. 130.7"],"checks":[{"q":"A class 0 rubber insulating glove has a maximum use voltage of","opts":["1,000 V","500 V","7,500 V","17,000 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"Class 00 is 500 V, class 0 is 1,000 V.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Glove inspection and testing","body":"Rubber insulating gloves are life-safety equipment. They need inspection before every use and electrical testing on a schedule.","warning":"Remove from service any glove that fails inspection. Do not patch or repair rubber insulating gloves.","step_list":["Visually inspect inside and out for cuts, punctures, embedded material, chemical damage and ozone cracking.","Do an air test by inflating the glove, or use a glove inflator, to find leaks.","Check the date and class markings, and the retest date.","Store in a glove bag, flat or hanging, away from sunlight, ozone and heat.","Have gloves electrically tested at a laboratory before first issue and then at intervals set by the standard, generally every six months while in use."],"refs":["OSHA 29 CFR 1910.137(b)","ASTM F496"],"checks":[{"q":"How often must rubber insulating gloves in service be electrically retested?","opts":["Generally every six months","Every ten years","Only when damaged","Every five years"],"correct":0,"explain":"Gloves are retested at intervals not exceeding six months in use.","ref":"1910.137"}]},{"title":"Insulated tools and test instruments","body":"Insulated hand tools and properly rated meters are part of the shock control. Use tools tested to the correct standard and meters rated for the circuit.","bullets":["Insulated hand tools should meet ASTM F1505 and be rated for 1,000 V.","Inspect insulation before each use. Damaged insulation means the tool comes out of service.","Test instruments must be rated for the environment and category: CAT III or CAT IV for building electrical systems, with voltage rating above the circuit.","Use test leads with finger guards and minimal exposed probe tips.","Check the meter on a known source before and after you test, the test-before-touch method."],"refs":["ASTM F1505","IEC 61010","NFPA 70E Art. 110","NFPA 70E Art. 120"],"checks":[{"q":"Before using a meter to verify a circuit is dead, you should","opts":["Test it on a known live source and again after","Test only after","Skip the check if new","Only check the batteries"],"correct":0,"explain":"Prove the meter works before and after the test.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Inspection and care of PPE","body":"All PPE needs inspection, cleaning and proper storage. Arc-rated clothing loses protection when contaminated or damaged.","bullets":["Inspect before each use; remove damaged items.","Follow manufacturer cleaning instructions; avoid bleach or fabric softeners that can harm arc-rated fabric unless allowed.","Keep clothing free from flammable contaminants such as oil and grease.","Repairs to arc-rated garments must use arc-rated material and thread.","Store PPE in clean, dry places, out of sunlight."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.7","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.132(d)"],"checks":[{"q":"Which contaminates arc-rated clothing and reduces its protection?","opts":["Oil or grease","Clean water","Dry air","Room temperature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Flammable contaminants can ignite and make injuries worse.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Putting it together: choosing PPE","body":"Pick PPE after the assessment. Always work through shock first, then arc flash, then add the task-specific items.","info":"Example: a 480 V panel with a label showing 8 cal/cm2 at 18 in. Wear PPE with a minimum arc rating of at least 8 cal/cm2, with rubber gloves and leather protectors, an arc-rated face shield with balaclava, hearing protection and safety glasses.","step_list":["De-energize if possible. If you can establish an electrically safe work condition, the energized PPE is not needed for that task.","Do the shock risk assessment: voltage, boundaries, glove class and tools.","Do the arc flash risk assessment: incident energy at the working distance or PPE category.","Select arc-rated clothing and face protection with a rating at or above the value.","Add hearing, head, eye and foot protection.","Inspect everything before use. Brief the crew."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130"],"checks":[{"q":"A label shows 8 cal/cm2 at the working distance. Clothing with which minimum arc rating is required?","opts":["8 cal/cm2 or higher","1.2 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2 is enough","40 cal/cm2 only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arc rating must be at least the incident energy at the working distance.","ref":""}]}],"quiz":[{"q":"PPE is","opts":["The last line of defense after other controls","The first control to use","Unneeded if training is done","Equal to elimination"],"correct":0,"explain":"PPE is the least effective control.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex F"},{"q":"Arc rating is expressed in","opts":["cal/cm2","Amps","Volts","Watts"],"correct":0,"explain":"It is calories per square centimeter.","ref":"ASTM F1959"},{"q":"ATPV stands for","opts":["Arc Thermal Performance Value","Arc Test Pressure Value","Average Temperature Protection Value","Arc Time Protection Voltage"],"correct":0,"explain":"ATPV is the energy giving 50% probability of second-degree burn.","ref":"ASTM F1959"},{"q":"EBT is the incident energy at which","opts":["The fabric breaks open","The fabric melts","The wearer is burned","The clothing dries"],"correct":0,"explain":"Energy of Breakopen Threshold.","ref":"ASTM F1959"},{"q":"Which fiber should NOT be worn next to the skin in arc flash exposure?","opts":["Polyester","Cotton","Arc-rated cotton","Flame-resistant wool"],"correct":0,"explain":"Meltable synthetics can melt onto skin.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"A face shield used for arc flash must be","opts":["Arc-rated for the exposure","Clear and cheap","Tinted dark","Made of any plastic"],"correct":0,"explain":"Only arc-rated face protection protects against heat.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"Hearing protection is useful because arc blast","opts":["Is loud and can cause hearing damage","Is silent","Has no pressure","Only affects eyes"],"correct":0,"explain":"The pressure wave and noise can damage hearing.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"EH-rated footwear is","opts":["Secondary shock protection only","Primary shock protection","Arc flash protection","Not allowed"],"correct":0,"explain":"EH footwear never replaces rubber gloves.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"A class 00 rubber glove has a maximum use voltage of","opts":["500 V","1,000 V","7,500 V","17,000 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"Class 00 is 500 V.","ref":"ASTM D120"},{"q":"A class 2 rubber glove has a maximum use voltage of","opts":["17,000 V","1,000 V","500 V","36,000 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"Class 2 is 17,000 V.","ref":"ASTM D120"},{"q":"Leather protectors are worn over rubber gloves to","opts":["Protect the rubber from cuts and add arc flash protection","Increase voltage rating","Replace the air test","Make them heavier"],"correct":0,"explain":"Protectors guard the rubber.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"Rubber insulating gloves in service are typically electrically retested","opts":["About every six months","Every ten years","Never","Only if torn"],"correct":0,"explain":"Test intervals are generally six months in use.","ref":"1910.137"},{"q":"The air test of a glove is used to","opts":["Find pinholes and leaks","Measure voltage","Change class","Dry the glove"],"correct":0,"explain":"Inflate and listen for leaks.","ref":"1910.137"},{"q":"A damaged insulating glove should be","opts":["Removed from service","Patched with tape","Used for low voltage only","Worn inside out"],"correct":0,"explain":"Never patch rubber insulating gloves.","ref":"1910.137"},{"q":"Insulated hand tools for 1,000 V work should meet","opts":["ASTM F1505","UL 94","ANSI Z87","A paint standard"],"correct":0,"explain":"F1505 is the insulated tool standard.","ref":"ASTM F1505"},{"q":"Before and after testing for absence of voltage you should","opts":["Verify the meter on a known live source","Skip the test","Only wipe the meter","Use any meter"],"correct":0,"explain":"Test the tester.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Repairs to arc-rated clothing must use","opts":["Arc-rated materials and thread","Any fabric","Duct tape","Staples"],"correct":0,"explain":"Repairs must not reduce protection.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"},{"q":"A label shows 8 cal/cm2. Clothing must be rated at least","opts":["8 cal/cm2","1.2 cal/cm2","4 cal/cm2","0 cal/cm2"],"correct":0,"explain":"Arc rating must meet or exceed the incident energy.","ref":"NFPA 70E 130.7"}]},{"title":"Safe Work Practices: Lockout, Verification, Permits and Planning","minutes":60,"overview":["Establishing an electrically safe work condition","Lockout/tagout and testing for absence of voltage","When energized work is justified and the permit","Job briefings and emergency response","Case studies and course review"],"slides":[{"title":"Establishing an electrically safe work condition","body":"The strongest safe work practice is to remove the hazard by de-energizing. An electrically safe work condition is a state in which the conductor or circuit part has been disconnected, locked or tagged, tested and, where needed, grounded.","keypoint":"Until all steps are complete the equipment is treated as energized and the energized work rules apply.","step_list":["Determine all possible sources of electrical supply: drawings, labels, field checks, including back-feed, control power and capacitors.","After interrupting the load current, open the disconnecting devices.","Where possible, visually verify that all blades are open or draw-out devices are fully withdrawn.","Apply lockout/tagout devices following a documented procedure.","Test each phase conductor or circuit part with an adequately rated test instrument to verify absence of voltage. Check the instrument on a known source before and after.","Where the possibility of induced voltage or stored energy exists, apply temporary protective grounding."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 120","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333(b)"],"checks":[{"q":"Equipment is considered de-energized for work only after","opts":["All the steps of establishing an electrically safe work condition are complete","The breaker is open","A tag is hung","The panel feels cool"],"correct":0,"explain":"Opening a breaker is only one step. The process must be completed, including testing.","ref":"Art. 120"}]},{"title":"Lockout and tagout","body":"Lockout devices hold a disconnect in the open position with a key or combination lock. Tagout devices are a warning. Where a device can be locked, lockout is generally the standard; tagout alone is allowed only when the device cannot be locked, with additional protections.","info":"For electrical utilization installations, OSHA's lockout/tagout requirements are in 1910.333(b)(2) of Subpart S, not in the general lockout rule 1910.147, which excludes work covered by Subpart S. Utilities follow 1910.269.","bullets":["Each worker applies their own personal lock. One person, one lock, one key.","Locks and tags identify the person who applied them. Only the person who applied the device removes it, except under a documented procedure for emergencies.","Group lockout uses a lock box so that each member has a lock on the box.","Tags that are not backed by a lock must be backed by additional measures that give equal safety.","Do not use the lock or tag as the only proof the equipment is dead. Verify."],"refs":["OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147","NFPA 70E Art. 120"],"checks":[{"q":"For electrical work in a utilization installation, OSHA's lockout/tagout rules are mainly found in","opts":["1910.333(b)(2)","1910.1200","1926.1053","1910.95"],"correct":0,"explain":"Subpart S covers electrical lockout/tagout; 1910.147 excludes work covered by Subpart S.","ref":"1910.333(b)(2)"}]},{"title":"Verifying absence of voltage","body":"Verification proves the circuit is dead. The method is \"test before touch\" with a meter you have proven works.","warning":"A tester that shows no voltage because its battery is dead or its leads are open is a classic cause of fatal incidents.","info":"The 2024 edition requires testing each phase conductor or circuit part at each point of work. The 2027 edition reportedly adds extra testing methods when absence-of-voltage testing alone is not enough, such as current transformer secondaries. Confirm in the published text.","step_list":["Choose a test instrument rated for the voltage and category of the circuit.","Check the instrument on a known live source (or a proving unit) to be sure it works.","Test every phase conductor or circuit part at each point of work, phase to phase and phase to ground.","Check the instrument again on the known source after testing.","Verify the instrument is working before and after each test, and treat the circuit as energized if the meter fails."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 120","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)"],"checks":[{"q":"After a voltage test shows no voltage, you should","opts":["Check the meter again on a known live source","Start work immediately","Take the lock off","Skip further checks"],"correct":0,"explain":"A second check confirms the meter did not fail during the test.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Stored energy and grounding","body":"Disconnecting power does not always remove every hazard. Stored energy, back-feed and induced voltage can remain.","bullets":["Capacitors can hold a charge. Discharge and short them as required.","Springs in breaker operators must be released or blocked.","Back-feed can come from generators, solar, batteries or other sources.","Parallel circuits and nearby energized lines can induce voltage on de-energized conductors.","Temporary protective grounds, applied after testing, can protect the worker where induced voltage is possible.","Neutrals can carry current after phase conductors are de-energized, so treat them carefully."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 120"],"checks":[{"q":"Which can still present a hazard after a feeder breaker is opened and locked out?","opts":["Stored energy in a capacitor bank","Only the breaker handle","The panel color","Nothing, it is dead"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stored energy and back-feed are not removed by opening the supply.","ref":""}]},{"title":"When energized work is justified","body":"Energized work is allowed only if de-energizing creates a greater hazard or is infeasible. Cost or inconvenience alone is not a justification.","table":{"h":["Justified","Not justified"],"r":[["De-energizing would create additional or increased hazards, for example shutting down life support, hazardous location ventilation or emergency alarms","The customer does not want an outage"],["Infeasible due to equipment design or operational limitations, for example work on circuits that are part of a continuous process that cannot be shut down","It takes longer to de-energize"],["Testing, troubleshooting and voltage measurement, which require energized circuits and have their own rules","The task is quick"]]},"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.2","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333(a)(1)"],"checks":[{"q":"Which is NOT a valid reason to work on energized equipment?","opts":["The customer would prefer not to have an outage","De-energizing would shut down life-support equipment","De-energizing is infeasible due to equipment design","The work is troubleshooting that requires voltage"],"correct":0,"explain":"Convenience is not justification.","ref":"1910.333(a)(1)"}]},{"title":"The energized electrical work permit","body":"When energized work is justified and the task is not exempt, an energized electrical work permit documents the plan. Management or an authorized person approves it.","info":"Some tasks do not require a permit when a qualified person works within the limited approach boundary with shock controls in place, such as testing and voltage measurement. Check the exemption wording in your edition.","bullets":["Circuit, equipment and location","Justification for why work cannot be done de-energized","Description of the safe work practices to be used","Results of the shock risk assessment","Results of the arc flash risk assessment, including incident energy or PPE category and the arc flash boundary","Means to keep unqualified persons away","Evidence of the job briefing, including emergency procedures","Approval signatures"],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.2"],"checks":[{"q":"The permit must include","opts":["The justification for working energized","The employee's salary","The cost of PPE","The customer phone number"],"correct":0,"explain":"Justification is one of the required items.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Job briefing and job safety planning","body":"Before work starts, the crew meets for a job briefing. The briefing covers hazards, procedures, controls, PPE, and emergency plans.","keypoint":"Any worker can stop the job if conditions change or if something does not look right.","step_list":["Describe the task and who is involved.","Identify energy sources and how they are controlled.","Review shock and arc flash boundaries and the PPE required.","Assign roles: who tests, who watches, who calls for help.","Review the emergency plan and the location of AED and shutoffs.","Confirm everyone understands and can stop the job."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 110","NFPA 70E Art. 130"],"checks":[{"q":"A job briefing should include","opts":["Hazards, procedures, controls, PPE and emergency plan","Only the schedule","Only PPE","Only the budget"],"correct":0,"explain":"The briefing covers the whole safety plan.","ref":""}]},{"title":"Case study: troubleshooting a motor control center","body":"A crew must find why a conveyor won't start. The motor control center is a 480 V MCC with labels showing 8.4 cal/cm2 at 18 in. The plant says it can't shut down the line.","keypoint":"Troubleshooting energized does not mean repairing energized. Once you know what to fix, de-energize.","step_list":["Is it justified? Troubleshooting that requires voltage is a recognized energized task, but the crew should first see whether parts of the work can be done with the bucket de-energized.","Shock and arc flash risk assessments are done. Boundaries and PPE are set: arc-rated clothing rated at least 8.4 cal/cm2, rubber gloves with protectors, arc-rated face shield and balaclava, hearing protection and safety glasses.","The crew uses insulated tools and a CAT III meter, proven on a known source before and after.","A job briefing is held; one worker is assigned as safety observer with training in release and emergency response.","Once the fault is located, the crew de-energizes and locks out to make the repair."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.333"]},{"title":"Case study: racking a breaker","body":"A worker racks in a 480 V breaker without remote racking while the breaker is closed. The breaker was in service, and the arc flash label shows 25 cal/cm2.","warning":"A breaker that is closed under load should never be moved. The breaker should be opened using its own controls before racking.","bullets":["What went wrong: racking a breaker is a task associated with higher arc flash likelihood. Racking a breaker that is closed into a live bus is not a safe practice.","Better control: open the breaker first, use remote racking so the worker is outside the arc flash boundary, and follow the manufacturer's procedure.","PPE: if the worker must be inside the boundary, arc-rated protection rated for the incident energy, category 3 or higher, hearing protection and gloves as needed.","Administrative: permit, briefing, and an observer."],"refs":["NFPA 70E Art. 130.5","NFPA 70E Annex F"]},{"title":"Course review: five rules to remember","body":"Safe electrical work comes down to a small number of habits that you apply every time.","info":"This course summarizes the NFPA 70E 2024 edition and OSHA rules for training purposes. Always use the edition your employer has adopted and the text of the standard, and follow your employer's electrical safety program.","step_list":["De-energize first. Establish an electrically safe work condition whenever possible.","Test before touch. Prove your meter, then test every conductor.","Know the boundaries and the hazard. Use the risk assessment and label information.","Match PPE to the hazard and inspect it every time.","Plan the work and the rescue. Brief the crew and know who can stop the job."],"refs":["NFPA 70E","OSHA 29 CFR 1910 Subpart S"]}],"quiz":[{"q":"The first step in establishing an electrically safe work condition is to","opts":["Determine all possible sources of electrical supply","Wear PPE","Hang a tag","Start the job briefing"],"correct":0,"explain":"You can't de-energize a source you haven't identified.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"After opening the disconnects and applying locks, the next critical step is","opts":["Test each conductor for absence of voltage","Remove the lock","Assume it is dead","Start work"],"correct":0,"explain":"Verify with a tested meter.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Each worker on a lockout should","opts":["Apply their own lock","Share one lock","Use only tags","Rely on the supervisor's lock"],"correct":0,"explain":"One person, one lock, one key.","ref":"OSHA 1910.333(b)"},{"q":"Tagout alone is used when","opts":["The device cannot be locked, with added protection","The job is short","The meter works","The panel is new"],"correct":0,"explain":"Lockout is preferred.","ref":"OSHA 1910.333(b)"},{"q":"Electrical lockout for utilization installations is covered by","opts":["29 CFR 1910.333(b)(2)","29 CFR 1910.95","29 CFR 1904","29 CFR 1926.20"],"correct":0,"explain":"Subpart S covers it.","ref":"1910.333"},{"q":"When testing for absence of voltage, the meter must be","opts":["Verified on a known source before and after the test","Brand new","Left in the truck","Used only once"],"correct":0,"explain":"Prove the meter works.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Which can remain after a circuit is disconnected?","opts":["Stored energy in capacitors","Nothing","Only paint","Only labels"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stored energy needs to be released.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Temporary protective grounds are used","opts":["After verifying absence of voltage, where induced or back-feed voltage is possible","Before locking out","In place of testing","Only on 120 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"They protect against inadvertent re-energization.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Energized work is permitted when","opts":["De-energizing introduces additional hazards or is infeasible","The customer prefers it","It saves time","It costs less"],"correct":0,"explain":"Cost and convenience are not valid justifications.","ref":"1910.333(a)(1)"},{"q":"Which is an example of a valid reason to work energized?","opts":["De-energizing would shut down life-support equipment","It takes too long to schedule","The crew is in a hurry","No locks available"],"correct":0,"explain":"Creating a greater hazard is justification.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130.2"},{"q":"An energized electrical work permit must include","opts":["The results of the shock and arc flash risk assessments","The weather","Employee age","Lunch plans"],"correct":0,"explain":"The permit documents the risk assessments.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130.2"},{"q":"A job briefing should be repeated","opts":["When the job changes","Never","Only annually","Only for new hires"],"correct":0,"explain":"Changes need new briefings.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"Troubleshooting energized equipment means","opts":["You should de-energize before making repairs when possible","You should repair live","You can skip PPE","You can skip the briefing"],"correct":0,"explain":"Troubleshoot energized only as needed; repair de-energized.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130"},{"q":"A closed breaker should be","opts":["Opened before it is racked","Racked while closed","Left closed and racked","Tagged"],"correct":0,"explain":"Racking a closed breaker is unsafe.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 130.5"},{"q":"Remote racking is useful because it","opts":["Moves the worker outside the arc flash boundary","Eliminates all hazards","Saves paint","Changes the voltage"],"correct":0,"explain":"Distance reduces exposure.","ref":"NFPA 70E Annex F"},{"q":"Which person may stop a job in progress when conditions change?","opts":["Any worker on the job","Only the supervisor","Only the inspector","No one"],"correct":0,"explain":"Stop-work authority belongs to everyone.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 110"},{"q":"Which statement about a lock is true?","opts":["It does not replace testing for absence of voltage","It proves the circuit is dead","It replaces the meter","It is only needed for 480 V"],"correct":0,"explain":"A lock holds a device open; testing proves the circuit is dead.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"},{"q":"Which is a key habit from the course?","opts":["Test before touch with a meter you have proven works","Trust labels alone","Skip permits","Use cotton only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Test before touch is a core habit.","ref":"NFPA 70E Art. 120"}]}]};
